The URL [hash](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URL/hash) is a string that follows the `#` symbol in a URL. It is commonly used on websites to link to a specific section of a page, but it can also be used to store data. Expo Router treats the hash as a special search parameter using the name `#`. It can be accessed and modified using the same hooks and APIs from [search parameters](#local-versus-global-url-parameters).

```tsx src/app/hash.tsx
import { Text } from 'react-native';
import { router, useLocalSearchParams, Link } from 'expo-router';

export default function User() {
  // Access the hash
  const { '#': hash } = useLocalSearchParams<{ '#': string }>();

  return (
    <>
      <Text onPress={() => router.setParams({ '#': 'my-hash' })}>Set a new hash</Text>
      <Text onPress={() => router.push('/#my-hash')}>Push with a new hash</Text>
      <Link href="/#my-hash">Link with a hash</Link>
    </>
  );
}
```

Ensure your project is using server output, this will configure the export and production builds to generate a server bundle as well as the client bundle.

```json app.json
{
  "web": {
    /* @info Output a dynamic server. */
    "output": "server"
    /* @end */
  }
}
```

In SDK 58 and later, API routes are enabled by default with `web.output: "server"`. To combine API routes with HTML generated at build time, use `web.output: "static"` and enable `apiRoutes` in the `expo-router` config plugin:

```json app.json
{
  "expo": {
    "web": {
      "output": "static"
    },
    "plugins": [["expo-router", { "apiRoutes": true }]]
  }
}
```

This exports prerendered HTML and API routes to **dist/server**, and client assets to **dist/client**. It requires a [deployed server](#deployment).

Set `apiRoutes` to `false` to disable API routes while keeping your selected rendering mode. Enabling API routes requires `static` or `server` output. An explicit `apiRoutes: false` is accepted in any output mode.

An API route is created in the **app** directory. For example, add the following route handler. It is executed when the route `/hello` is matched.

```ts src/app/hello+api.ts
export function GET(request: Request) {
  return Response.json({ hello: 'world' });
}
```

You can export any of the following functions `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`, `HEAD`, and `OPTIONS` from a server route. The function executes when the corresponding HTTP method is matched. Unsupported methods will automatically return `405: Method not allowed`.

