In SDK 58 and later, you can opt into experimental granular bundle splitting for production web exports. By default, Expo collects dependencies shared by multiple async routes into a common chunk. Granular splitting groups shared dependencies by the routes that need them, so a route can download only the modules that it uses.

Set `experiments.chunking.mode` to `granular` in your app config and keep async routes enabled on web:

```json app.json
{
  "expo": {
    "experiments": {
      "chunking": { "mode": "granular" }
    },
    "plugins": [["expo-router", { "asyncRoutes": { "web": true } }]]
  }
}
```

Granular splitting applies only to production web client exports with bundle splitting enabled. Development bundles, native bundles, and DOM component bundles continue to use the legacy strategy. Projects that enable React Server Components use the legacy strategy for every bundle. If a web worker or one of its dependencies contains an asynchronous import, Expo falls back to legacy splitting for the entire bundle graph.

Remove `experiments.chunking` or set its `mode` to `legacy` to restore the default strategy. This setting is separate from `asyncRoutes`: enabling async routes does not opt into granular splitting.

